<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cimon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 510–450 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cimon was an Athenian statesman and general, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miltiades">Miltiades</a>, who led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a> in the decades after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greco-persian-wars">Persian Wars</a>. Around 466 BCE he destroyed a Persian fleet and army at the Eurymedon river, and in 463 BCE he reduced rebellious Thasos. A supporter of friendship with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, he was ostracised in 461 BCE but later recalled, and he died on campaign in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> about 450 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cimon.jpg" alt="Cimon" width="320" height="481" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Markus Leupold-Löwenthal · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KimonSculpture.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Cimon was born into the Athenian nobility, a member of the Philaidae clan from the deme of Laciadae. His grandfather, Cimon Coalemos, won three Olympic victories with his four-horse <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chariot">chariot</a> and was assassinated by the sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peisistratus">Peisistratus</a>. His mother was Hegesipyle, daughter of the Thracian king Olorus. When his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miltiades">Miltiades</a> was fined 50 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talent">talents</a> after an accusation of treason, he could not pay and died in prison in 489 BCE. Cimon inherited the debt. As head of the household he also had charge of his sister or half-sister Elpinice, and in his youth he had a reputation as a hard drinker, blunt and unrefined.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Cimon distinguished himself at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-salamis">Battle of Salamis</a> and was a member of an embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> in 479 BCE. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delian-league">Delian League</a> was formed, he was agreed to be its principal commander, and he directed most of its operations until 463 BCE. He captured Eion on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/struma">Strymon</a> from the Persian general Boges, conquered Scyros and drove out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> based there, and brought back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> the bones said to be those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theseus">Theseus</a>. Around 466 BCE he defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> at the Eurymedon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamphylia">Pamphylia</a>, capturing their camp and destroying or taking a fleet of 200 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a>. He founded Amphipolis in 465 BCE with 10,000 settlers.</p><p>Between 465 and 463 BCE he besieged Thasos, which had left the League over a trade rivalry and the ownership of a gold mine. He was then prosecuted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a> for allegedly taking bribes from Alexander I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>, and was acquitted. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>'s proxenos at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, he persuaded the Assembly in 462 BCE to send 4,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a> to help Sparta against its rebellious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a>. The Spartans, fearing that the Athenians would side with the helots, sent them home. The snub ended his popularity, and he was ostracised for ten years from 461 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In his absence Ephialtes, with the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pericles">Pericles</a>, reduced the power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/areopagus">Council of the Areopagus</a>, and some of Cimon's policies were reversed. Cimon returned around 451 BCE and negotiated a five-year truce with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. He then proposed an expedition against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and sailed with two hundred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trireme">triremes</a>. He besieged Citium and died during or soon after the failed siege in 450 BCE, of unrecorded causes. His death was kept secret from the army, which won a victory at Salamis-in-Cyprus. He was buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>.</p><p>He had also paid for building in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, including the expansion of the Acropolis and the city walls. His domestic policy was consistently antidemocratic and failed, but his foreign policy rested on continued resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and on Athens as the leading sea power and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> the leading land power. That arrangement probably delayed the outbreak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cimon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Naval power, rowed largely by the poorest citizens, the thetes, became the basis of Athenian strength and of the Delian League: under <b>Cimon</b> the allies destroyed a Persian fleet and army at the Battle of the Eurymedon around 466 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>Cimon</b> the league expelled the remaining Persian forces from the Aegean, and in its first ten years Cimon forced Karystos in Euboea to join, conquered the island of Skyros and settled Athenian colonists there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delian-league">Delian League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>They had to win votes in the Assembly and account for every office they held, and a leader the people feared could be ostracised, as <b>Cimon</b> was in 461 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/societies/athens">Social Hierarchy of Classical Athens</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 466 BCE the Athenian general <b>Cimon</b> defeated a Persian fleet and army near the Eurymedon.</blockquote><a href="/state/aspendos">Aspendos</a></li><li><blockquote>When Athens challenged its mainland mines, Thasos revolted in 465 BCE and surrendered to <b>Cimon</b> after a two-year siege, losing its fleet and walls.</blockquote><a href="/state/thasos">Thasos</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Classical Athens, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.97,23.72&amp;year=-471" aria-label="Classical Athens on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5141 799 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Cimon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5591 1069h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5591 1069h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.97,23.72&amp;year=-471">What was here in 471 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
